ed for a \\(2025\\times2025\\) grid is \\(2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048\\).\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nFor each row \\(i\\;(1\\le i\\le n)\\) denote by \\(\\sigma(i)\\) the column of the unique uncovered unit square in that row.  \nBecause each column must also contain exactly one uncovered square, \\(\\sigma\\) is a permutation of \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Counting covered intervals in rows  \n\nIn row \\(i\\) the covered cells are all columns except \\(\\sigma(i)\\).  \nHence the set of covered cells in row \\(i\\) is the union of the (possibly empty) intervals  \n\n\\[\nL_i=[1,\\sigma(i)-1],\\qquad R_i=[\\sigma(i)+1,n].\n\\]\n\nThus row \\(i\\) contributes  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\n1 &\\text{if }\\sigma(i)=1\\text{ or }\\sigma(i)=n,\\\\[2mm]\n2 &\\text{otherwise.}\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\sigma\\) is a permutation, exactly one row has \\(\\sigma(i)=1\\) and exactly one row has \\(\\sigma(i)=n\\).  \nConsequently the total number of covered intervals over all rows is  \n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{2\\,(n-2)}_{\\text{rows with interior holes}}\n\\;+\\;\\underbrace{1+1}_{\\text{two endpoint rows}}\n\\;=\\;2n-2. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Relating intervals to the tiles  \n\nAssume a tiling consists of \\(m\\) rectangular tiles.  \nFor the \\(k\\)-th tile let  \n\n\\[\nh_k=\\text{its height (number of rows it occupies)},\\qquad\nw_k=\\text{its width (number of columns it occupies)} .\n\\]\n\nA tile intersecting a given row occupies a single contiguous block of columns; therefore it contributes exactly one covered interval to that row.  \nHence a tile of height \\(h_k\\) contributes \\(h_k\\) row‑intervals (one for each of its rows).  \nSumming over all tiles we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{k=1}^{m} h_k \\;=\\; \\text{total number of row‑intervals}\n\\;\\ge\\;2n-2 \\quad\\text{by (1).} \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nT