length of row \\(\\sigma(k)\\) equals \\(c_{\\sigma(k)}-1\\), and these lengths are strictly increasing from \\(0\\) up to \\(n-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Lemma.**  \nLet a Ferrers diagram have rows of lengths \\(a_1<a_2<\\dots <a_m\\) (all distinct and positive). Any tiling of the diagram by axis‑parallel rectangles whose sides lie on the grid lines uses at least \\(m\\) rectangles.\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider the columns where the height of the diagram changes; there are exactly \\(m\\) such columns, namely the columns numbered \\(a_1+1,a_2+1,\\dots ,a_m+1\\).  \nIn a column whose height changes, a rectangle can occupy cells only on one side of the change; otherwise the rectangle would contain a cell that does not belong to the diagram.  \nHence each change of height forces a distinct rectangle, giving at least \\(m\\) rectangles. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nApplying the lemma to the left region:\n\n- The row lengths are \\(0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\); discarding the unique zero‑length row leaves \\(n-1\\) distinct positive lengths.\n- Therefore any tiling of the left region needs at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\nThe right region, after the same ordering of rows, is the transpose of the left region and has the same set of distinct positive lengths. Consequently it also requires at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\nHence **any** admissible tiling of the whole board needs at least  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Construction achieving the bound  \n\nChoose the permutation \\(p(i)=i\\) (the main diagonal).  \nFor each integer \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\) define two rectangles:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nR_k &:= \\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le k,\\; k+1\\le j\\le n\\},\\\\[2pt]\nS_k &:= \\{(i,j)\\mid k+1\\le i\\le n,\\; 1\\le j\\le k\\}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n*Verification.*  \n- In row \\(