oes not exist, contradicting the requirement that rectangles lie entirely inside the diagram. Consequently no single rectangle can cover cells on both sides of such a height\u2011change column.  \n\nSince there are \\(m\\) distinct height\u2011change columns, at least \\(m\\) different rectangles are needed to cover the whole diagram. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Lower bound for the original problem  \n\nApplying the lemma to the left region gives a lower bound of \\(n-1\\) rectangles, because the left Ferrers diagram has the distinct positive lengths \\(1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\).  \n\nThe right region, after the same ordering of rows, is the transpose of the left region; its row lengths are also \\(1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\). Hence it also requires at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.  \n\nSince the two regions are disjoint, any tiling of the whole board needs at least  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\nFor \\(n=2025\\) this lower bound equals \\(2\\cdot2025-2=4048\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Explicit tiling with \\(2n-2\\) rectangles  \n\nChoose the permutation \\(p(i)=i\\); the uncovered squares are the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\).  \n\nFor each integer \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\) define two rectangles:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nR_k &:= \\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le k,\\; k+1\\le j\\le n\\},\\\\[2pt]\nS_k &:= \\{(i,j)\\mid k+1\\le i\\le n,\\; 1\\le j\\le k\\}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n*Coverage.*  \nTake any cell \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i\\neq j\\).  \n\n- If \\(i<j\\) then \\(j\\ge i+1\\); setting \\(k=i\\) we have \\(i\\le k\\) and \\(j\\ge k+1\\), so \\((i,j)\\in R_k\\).  \n- If \\(i>j\\) then \\(i\\ge j+1\\); setting \\(k=j\\) we have \\(i\\ge k+1\\) and \\(j\\le k\\), so \\((i,j)\\in S_k\\).\n\nThus every non\u2011diagonal cell belongs to exactly one of the rectangles.  \n\n*Disjointness.*  \nThe rectangles \\(R_k\\) lie entirely to the right of the diagonal, while the rectangles \\(S_k\\) lie entirely to the left; therefore no two rectangles intersect in interior points.  \n\nThe family \\(\\{R_k,S_k\\mid k=1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) consists of \\(2(n-1)\\) rectangles and covers all cells of \\(B\\setminus U\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Minimality  \n\nWe have exhibited a tiling using \\(2n-2\\) rectangles and proved that no tiling can use fewer. Hence the minimum possible number of tiles is exactly \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the given board (\\(n=2025\\)) the minimum is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]"
