the \\(a_i\\)\u2019s and at most one of\n   the \\(b_i\\)\u2019s can be missing.  Consequently there are exactly\n   \\(2(n-1)\\) such squares.)\n\n   **Claim.** No rectangle can contain two different squares among the set\n   \\(\\{a_i,b_i\\mid i=2,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n   *Proof of the claim.*  \n   Suppose a rectangle \\(R\\) contains two distinct squares of this set.\n   Because the sides of a rectangle are aligned with the grid, the rows and\n   columns occupied by \\(R\\) are intervals.  Let the two squares be\n   \\(a_i=(i,\\pi(i)-1)\\) and \\(a_j=(j,\\pi(j)-1)\\) with \\(i<j\\) (the case\n   involving a \\(b\\)-square is analogous).  \n   Since both squares lie in the same column interval, the whole column\n   \\(\\pi(i)-1\\) belongs to \\(R\\).  The row interval of \\(R\\) contains the rows\n   \\(i\\) and \\(j\\); therefore it also contains the intermediate row\n   \\(k\\) with \\(i<k<j\\).  Consequently the cell \\((k,\\pi(i)-1)\\) is inside\n   \\(R\\).  But the uncovered square in row \\(k\\) is \\((k,\\pi(k))\\); because\n   \\(\\pi\\) is a permutation, \\(\\pi(k)\\neq\\pi(i)-1\\).  Hence the cell\n   \\((k,\\pi(i)-1)\\) is a *covered* square, which is allowed.  The problem\n   appears only when the column interval of \\(R\\) also contains the column\n   \\(\\pi(k)\\).  However, the column interval of \\(R\\) contains\n   \\(\\pi(i)-1\\) and \\(\\pi(j)-1\\); since \\(\\pi(i)\\neq\\pi(j)\\), the interval\n   must contain at least the two distinct columns \\(\\pi(i)-1\\) and\n   \\(\\pi(j)-1\\).  Between them lies the column \\(\\pi(k)\\) for some\n   \\(k\\in\\{i,\\dots ,j\\}\\) (by the pigeon\u2011hole principle applied to the set\n   \\(\\{\\pi(i),\\dots ,\\pi(j)\\}\\)).  Consequently the uncovered square\n   \\((k,\\pi(k))\\) lies inside \\(R\\), contradicting the requirement that a\n   rectangle may not cover an uncovered square.  \u220e\n\n   Because each rectangle can contain **at most one** of the \\(2(n-1)\\)\n   distinguished squares, at least \\(2(n-1)\\) rectangles are necessary to\n   cover all of them, and therefore to cover the whole set of covered squares.\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   The construction shows that \\(2(n-1)\\) tiles are sufficient, and the\n   argument above shows that no tiling can use fewer than \\(2(n-1)\\) tiles.\n   Hence the minimum number of tiles is exactly \\(2(n-1)\\).\n\n   For the given grid \\(n=2025\\),\n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{4048}=2\\,(2025-1)\n   \\]\n\n   tiles are required.\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nA placement of tiles satisfying the condition \u201cexactly one uncovered unit\nsquare in each row and each column\u2019\u2019 forces the uncovered squares to be a set\n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\pi(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\pi\\) is a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \nAll other unit squares must be covered, and a tile is an axis\u2011parallel\nrectangle whose interior consists of a Cartesian product of a **contiguous**\nset of rows and a **contiguous** set of columns.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A construction using \\(2(n-1)\\) tiles\n\nChoose the identity permutation \\(\\pi(i)=i\\).  \nFor each \\(k=1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\) define two rectangles\n\n\\[\nU_k=\\{(r,c)\\mid 1\\le r\\le k,\\;k+1\\le c\\le n\\},\n\\qquad\nL_k=\\{(r,c)\\mid k+1\\le r\\le n,\\;1\\le c\\le k\\}.\n\\]\n\nBoth \\(U_k\\) and \\(L_k\\) avoid the diagonal squares \\((i,i)\\); they are\ncontiguous in rows and columns, hence are admissible tiles.  \nThe family\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{T}= \\{U_k,L_k\\mid k=1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(2(n-1)\\) rectangles.  Every non\u2011diagonal cell \\((r,c)\\)\nbelongs either to a unique \\(U_k\\) (if \\(r\\le c\\) and \\(r\\neq c\\)) or to a\nunique \\(L_k\\) (if \\(r>c\\)).  Consequently the \\(2(n-1)\\) tiles cover all\nunit squares except the diagonal ones, and each row and each column has\nexactly one uncovered square.  Hence\n\n\\[\n\\text{minimum number of tiles}\\le 2(n-1).\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A lower bound of \\(2(n-1)\\) tiles\n\nFix an arbitrary permutation \\(\\pi\\) and let \\(U\\) be the corresponding set\nof uncovered squares.  For each \\(i\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) define\n\n\\[\na_i=(i,\\pi(i)-1)\\quad\\text{(if }\\pi(i)>1\\text{)},\n\\qquad\nb_i=(i-1,\\pi(i))\\quad\\text{(if }\\pi(i)<n\\text{)} .\n\\]\n\nBecause the columns \\(\\pi(1),\\dots ,\\pi(n)\\) are all distinct, at most one\nof the \\(a_i\\) and at most one of the \\(b_i\\) can be missing; therefore the\nset\n\n\\[\nS=\\{a_i,b_i\\mid i=2,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(2(n-1)\\) unit squares.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma  \n\nNo admissible rectangle can contain two distinct squares of \\(S\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume a rectangle \\(R\\) contains two different squares of \\(S\\).  Because\nthe rows and columns of a rectangle are intervals, the rows of the two\nsquares form an interval \\([r_1,r_2]\\) and the columns form an interval\n\\([c_1,c_2]\\).  Without loss of generality the two squares are\n\\(a_i=(i,\\pi(i)-1)\\) and \\(a_j=(j,\\pi(j)-1)\\) with \\(i<j\\) (the other\npossibilities are analogous).  Then \\(c_1=\\pi(i)-1\\) and \\(c_2=\\pi(j)-1\\).\n\nConsider any integer \\(k\\) with \\(i\\le k\\le j\\).  Because the column\ninterval \\([c_1,c_2]\\) contains all integers between \\(\\pi(i)-1\\) and\n\\(\\pi(j)-1\\), it also contains the column \\(\\pi(k)\\) (the set\n\\(\\{\\pi(i),\\dots ,\\pi(j)\\}\\) consists of \\(j-i+1\\) distinct numbers, so one\nof them must lie between \\(\\pi(i)\\) and \\(\\pi(j)\\)).  Hence the cell\n\\((k,\\pi(k))\\) \u2013 which is an uncovered square \u2013 lies inside \\(R\\), contradicting\nthe definition of a tile.  \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nBecause each rectangle can contain **at most one** element of \\(S\\), any\ncovering of all squares must use at least \\(|S|=2(n-1)\\) rectangles.  Hence\n\n\\[\n\\text{minimum number of tiles}\\ge 2(n-1).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Minimality\n\nFrom (1) and (2) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\text{minimum number of tiles}=2(n-1).\n\\]\n\nFor the original problem \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}=2\\,(2025-1).\n\\]\n\nThus Matilda can achieve the required condition with 4048 rectangular tiles,\nand it is impossible to do it with fewer tiles."
