"### Summary  \n\n**a. Verdict:** I have found a complete solution. The minimum possible number of tiles is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n\n1. **Row\u2011interval count.**  \n   \u2013 In each row the uncovered cell splits the covered cells into at most two contiguous intervals.  \n   \u2013 Because the uncovered cells form a permutation of the columns, exactly one row has its hole in column\u202f1 and one row has its hole in column\u202f\\(n\\).  \n   \u2013 Hence the total number of covered intervals over all rows equals \\(2n-2\\).\n\n2. **From intervals to a lower bound.**  \n   \u2013 A tile of height \\(h_k\\) contributes exactly \\(h_k\\) row\u2011intervals (one per row it occupies). Summing over all tiles gives \\(\\sum h_k \\ge 2n-2\\).  \n   \u2013 By symmetry, \\(\\sum w_k \\ge 2n-2\\) for the column\u2011intervals.\n\n3. **Combining the inequalities.**  \n   \u2013 Adding yields \\(\\sum (h_k+w_k) \\ge 4n-4\\).  \n   \u2013 Since each tile satisfies \\(h_k\\ge1,\\;w_k\\ge1\\), we have \\(h_k+w_k\\ge2\\). Therefore  \n\n\\[\n2m=\\sum (h_k+w_k)\\ge4n-4\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad m\\ge2n-2 .\n\\]\n\n   This gives a universal lower bound of \\(2n-2\\) tiles.\n\n4. **Construction attaining the bound.**  \n   \u2013 Choose the uncovered squares to be the main diagonal: the hole in row \\(i\\) is cell \\((i,i)\\).  \n   \u2013 For each row \\(i\\) (\\(2\\le i\\le n-1\\)) place two horizontal \\(1\\times(i-1)\\) and \\(1\\times(n-i)\\) tiles covering columns \\(1,\\dots,i-1\\) and \\(i+1,\\dots,n\\), respectively.  \n   \u2013 In row\u202f1 place a single \\(1\\times(n-1)\\) tile covering columns \\(2,\\dots,n\\); in row\u202f\\(n\\) place a single \\(1\\times(n-1)\\) tile covering columns \\(1,\\dots,n-1\\).  \n\n   The total number of tiles is \\((n-2)\\times2+2 = 2n-2\\), which meets the lower bound.  \n   Every row and every column now has exactly one uncovered cell (the diagonal), and tiles are non\u2011overlapping rectangles aligned with the grid.\n\nThus the minimum number of tiles required for a \\(2025\\times2025\\) grid is \\(2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048\\).\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nFor each row \\(i\\;(1\\le i\\le n)\\) denote by \\(\\sigma(i)\\) the column of the unique uncovered unit square in that row.  \nBecause each column must also contain exactly one uncovered square, \\(\\sigma\\) is a permutation of \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Counting covered intervals in rows  \n\nIn row \\(i\\) the covered cells are all columns except \\(\\sigma(i)\\).  \nHence the set of covered cells in row \\(i\\) is the union of the (possibly empty) intervals  \n\n\\[\nL_i=[1,\\sigma(i)-1],\\qquad R_i=[\\sigma(i)+1,n].\n\\]\n\nThus row \\(i\\) contributes  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\n1 &\\text{if }\\sigma(i)=1\\text{ or }\\sigma(i)=n,\\\\[2mm]\n2 &\\text{otherwise.}\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\sigma\\) is a permutation, exactly one row has \\(\\sigma(i)=1\\) and exactly one row has \\(\\sigma(i)=n\\).  \nConsequently the total number of covered intervals over all rows is  \n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{2\\,(n-2)}_{\\text{rows with interior holes}}\n\\;+\\;\\underbrace{1+1}_{\\text{two endpoint rows}}\n\\;=\\;2n-2. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Relating intervals to the tiles  \n\nAssume a tiling consists of \\(m\\) rectangular tiles.  \nFor the \\(k\\)-th tile let  \n\n\\[\nh_k=\\text{its height (number of rows it occupies)},\\qquad\nw_k=\\text{its width (number of columns it occupies)} .\n\\]\n\nA tile intersecting a given row occupies a single contiguous block of columns; therefore it contributes exactly one covered interval to that row.  \nHence a tile of height \\(h_k\\) contributes \\(h_k\\) row\u2011intervals (one for each of its rows).  \nSumming over all tiles we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{k=1}^{m} h_k \\;=\\; \\text{total number of row\u2011intervals}\n\\;\\ge\\;2n-2 \\quad\\text{by (1).} \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe same reasoning applied to columns gives\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{k=1}^{m} w_k \\;\\ge\\;2n-2. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nAdding (2) and (3) yields\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{k=1}^{m}(h_k+w_k)\\;\\ge\\;4n-4. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Deriving a lower bound on the number of tiles  \n\nEach tile satisfies \\(h_k\\ge1\\) and \\(w_k\\ge1\\); therefore \\(h_k+w_k\\ge2\\).  \nSumming this inequality over all \\(m\\) tiles gives  \n\n\\[\n2m \\;=\\; \\sum_{k=1}^{m}(h_k+w_k)\\;\\ge\\;4n-4.\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nm \\;\\ge\\; 2n-2. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nEquation (5) is a universal lower bound for any admissible placement.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Construction attaining the bound  \n\nChoose the uncovered squares to be the main diagonal:\n\n\\[\n\\text{hole in row }i \\text{ is the cell } (i,i),\\qquad i=1,\\dots ,n .\n\\]\n\nFor each row \\(i\\) we place horizontal tiles as follows.\n\n* If \\(i=1\\): place a single tile of size \\(1\\times (n-1)\\) covering columns \\(2,3,\\dots ,n\\).\n* If \\(i=n\\): place a single tile of size \\(1\\times (n-1)\\) covering columns \\(1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\).\n* If \\(2\\le i\\le n-1\\): place two tiles, one of size \\(1\\times (i-1)\\) covering columns \\(1,\\dots ,i-1\\) and another of size \\(1\\times (n-i)\\) covering columns \\(i+1,\\dots ,n\\).\n\nAll tiles are rectangles whose sides lie on grid lines, they are pairwise disjoint, and together they cover every unit square except the diagonal cells.  \nConsequently each row and each column contains exactly one uncovered square (the diagonal one).\n\nThe number of tiles used is  \n\n\\[\n2\\;( \\text{rows }1\\text{ and }n) \\;+\\; 2\\,(n-2) \\;=\\; 2n-2 .\n\\]\n\nThus the bound (5) is tight.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Conclusion  \n\nThe minimal possible number of tiles for a \\(2025\\times2025\\) grid satisfying the given conditions is  \n\n\\[\n2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048.\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]"
